      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "The pedicled (in-situ) left internal mammary artery grafted to the left anterior descending artery has a very high late patency and reduces late mortality following coronary artery bypass surgery. A technique is described which achieves total arterial revascularization in patients with multivessel coronary disease and which is also entirely pedicled."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "Using the left internal mammary artery and radial artery joined as a composite Y graft, all coronary territories may be grafted."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "One in-hospital death from 464 patients (0.2%) occurred. Age (mean +/- standard error) was 64.7 +/- 0.5 years and number of distal anastomoses 3.4 +/- 0.04. Of 1,681 patients from Royal Melbourne Hospital, 346 had this operation. Comparison found no preoperative selection bias and no postoperative differences in complications. Actuarial survival was 0.98 +/- 0.01 at 36.1 +/- 0.3 months."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Total arterial revascularization may be performed using the left internal mammary artery and radial artery as a composite Y graft. There was no increase in complications. This technique preserves the left internal mammary artery to left anterior descending artery graft."
        }
      ]},
